+# Keep pickup reminders on SMS
+
+For volunteers considering a cheaper pickup reminder channel. Keep SMS because the email trial increased missed
+pickups and wasted fresh food, while many members do not check email.
+
+## Context
+
+SMS pickup reminders cost €40 a month. Larder tried email reminders from April to June 2025 to avoid that cost.
+The provider's report, as quoted in [issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md), said 34% of reminder emails were never
+opened. Missed pickups rose from 6% to 13% of pantry orders. Each missed pickup leaves a packed bag whose fresh
+food goes in the bin at closing time.
+
+The pantry lead reported that many members share one phone in their family and never check email, but see SMS.
+Larder switched back to SMS on July 1, 2025; missed pickups were back to 6% by August. When email was proposed
+again in issue 52, the maintainer confirmed that SMS would stay. It was the third such proposal that year.
+
+The choice must weigh the reminder bill against reaching members, completed pickups and fresh food waste.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Email reminders**: no sending cost, saving €40 a month. Rejected because the trial left many reminders unopened
+  and missed pickups rose to 13%, with fresh food discarded from uncollected bags.
+- **SMS reminders**: costs €40 a month and reaches members who do not check email. Chosen because missed pickups
+  returned to 6% after SMS resumed.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep pickup reminders on SMS. The observed improvement in pickups and the pantry lead's account of how members
+receive reminders justify the monthly sending cost over the savings from email.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The pantry continues to pay €40 a month for reminders. SMS supports pickups by members who do not check email;
+missed pickups still occur, with the reported rate at 6% after the switch back. A future proposal to change channels
+needs to address member reach, missed pickups and food waste as well as the sending cost.
